    "id": "cdr-a4",
    "type": "code",
    "title": "SQL: window functions on event data",
    "prompt": "Write SQL for three things over a table of events with columns customer_id, event_ts, amount and event_type: (1) the most recent event per customer; (2) a rolling seven-day sum of amount per customer at each event; (3) for each event, the number of prior events for that customer in the preceding hour, computed point-in-time correctly. State the assumptions and the trap in each.",
    "timeboxSec": 1500,
    "rubric": "Must-haves: (1) latest-per-customer implemented with ROW_NUMBER over a partition ordered by timestamp descending, filtered to 1 in an outer query or common table expression, with the tie trap named - equal timestamps produce an arbitrary winner unless a deterministic secondary ordering is added, and the difference between ROW_NUMBER, RANK and DENSE_RANK on ties stated; (2) the rolling sum written with a window frame, and the ROWS versus RANGE distinction made explicitly - a seven-day sum requires a RANGE frame over an interval, not a ROWS frame over a row count, unless one row per day is guaranteed; (3) the prior-events count written with a frame or inequality join bounded strictly before the current event, with the boundary strictness called out - whether the current row contributes to its own feature is decided by strict versus non-strict comparison, and it must be strict for point-in-time correctness; (4) common table expressions used per logical step rather than deep nesting, with readability named as deliberate; (5) assumptions stated: timestamp type and time zone, duplicate events, null amounts, and whether event_ts is event time or ingestion time; (6) at least one performance note - partitioning or indexing on customer and timestamp, or the cost of an inequality self-join versus a window frame. Bonus: connects the third query explicitly to point-in-time correct feature engineering and to leakage, and notes that using ingestion time rather than event time silently changes what the feature means. Common mistakes: GROUP BY with MAX for the latest row, which loses the other columns or requires a second join; a ROWS frame for a time-based window; a non-strict boundary that leaks the current event into its own feature; one deeply nested query; no assumptions. {{HONESTY}}",
    "model": "<p><b>Assumptions.</b> event_ts is a timestamp in a single time zone and represents event time rather than ingestion time - that distinction matters, because using ingestion time silently changes what every one of these features means. Duplicates are possible, amounts may be null, and I would confirm whether a null amount should be treated as zero or excluded.</p>\n<p><b>1. Most recent event per customer.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>WITH ranked AS (</code></li>\n<li><code>  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (</code></li>\n<li><code>    PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY event_ts DESC, event_id DESC) AS rn</code></li>\n<li><code>  FROM events)</code></li>\n<li><code>SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rn = 1;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap is ties. With equal timestamps, ROW_NUMBER picks arbitrarily and the result is not reproducible, so I add a deterministic secondary key. Also worth naming: RANK would give both tied rows rank 1 and return two rows; DENSE_RANK differs only in how it numbers subsequent groups. Using GROUP BY with MAX(event_ts) is the common wrong answer - it gives you the timestamp but not the rest of the row without a second join.</p>\n<p><b>2. Rolling seven-day sum per customer.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>SELECT customer_id, event_ts, amount,</code></li>\n<li><code>  SUM(amount) OVER (</code></li>\n<li><code>    PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY event_ts</code></li>\n<li><code>    RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL '7' DAY PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS sum_7d</code></li>\n<li><code>FROM events;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap is ROWS versus RANGE. A ROWS frame counts rows, so ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING would be the last seven events, not the last seven days - correct only if there is exactly one row per day. A time window needs a RANGE frame over an interval.</p>\n<p><b>3. Prior events in the preceding hour, point-in-time correct.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>SELECT customer_id, event_ts,</code></li>\n<li><code>  COUNT(*) OVER (</code></li>\n<li><code>    PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY event_ts</code></li>\n<li><code>    RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL '1' HOUR PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) - 1 AS prior_1h</code></li>\n<li><code>FROM events;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap here is the whole point of the question: the boundary. CURRENT ROW includes the event itself, so the count must exclude it - here by subtracting one, which is only correct if no other row shares the exact timestamp. If ties are possible, the safer form is an inequality self-join with a strict comparison on time, which makes the exclusion explicit rather than arithmetic.</p>\n<p>This is exactly point-in-time correct feature engineering. A feature that includes the event it is describing is leakage, and it produces a model that looks excellent offline and fails on the first day in production. Getting strict versus non-strict right on this boundary is the difference.</p>\n<p><b>Performance.</b> All three depend on ordering within a customer, so the table wants clustering or an index on customer_id and event_ts. The window-frame versions are single passes over the sorted partitions; the inequality self-join alternative is far more expensive, which is worth saying if someone proposes it.</p>"
